==============================================================================
(A) chi(R_m)=2 ?  Is the AP-rotational the unique 'barely cyclic' regular orbit?
==============================================================================
  m= 5: chi(R_m rotational/AP)=2  #3cyc=5;  chi(QR/Paley circulant)=1  #3cyc=0;  iso? False
  m= 7: chi(R_m rotational/AP)=2  #3cyc=14;  chi(QR/Paley circulant)=3  #3cyc=14;  iso? False
  m= 9: chi(R_m rotational/AP)=2  #3cyc=30;  chi(QR/Paley circulant)=3  #3cyc=30;  iso? False
  m=11: chi(R_m rotational/AP)=2  #3cyc=55;  chi(QR/Paley circulant)=4  #3cyc=55;  iso? n/a(m>9)
  m=13: chi(R_m rotational/AP)=2  #3cyc=91;  chi(QR/Paley circulant)=3  #3cyc=52;  iso? n/a(m>9)

==============================================================================
(B) which regular orbit do LRC TIGHT configs realize? (chi=2 AP vs chi=3 Paley)
==============================================================================
  n=6 AP {1..5}:  M=1/6(=0.1667), tight time 1/6
      [+e] regular=False  chi=2  #3cyc=3  ==R_m(AP,chi2)? False
      [-e] regular=True  chi=2  #3cyc=5  ==R_m(AP,chi2)? True
  n=8 AP {1..7}:  M=1/8(=0.1250), tight time 1/8
      [+e] regular=False  chi=2  #3cyc=11  ==R_m(AP,chi2)? False
      [-e] regular=True  chi=2  #3cyc=14  ==R_m(AP,chi2)? True
  n=8 sporadic1 {1,2,3,4,5,7,12}:  M=1/8(=0.1250), tight time 1/8
      [+e] regular=False  chi=2  #3cyc=8  ==R_m(AP,chi2)? False
      [-e] regular=False  chi=2  #3cyc=12  ==R_m(AP,chi2)? False
  n=8 sporadic2 {1,4,5,6,7,11,13}:  M=1/8(=0.1250), tight time 1/8
      [+e] regular=False  chi=2  #3cyc=11  ==R_m(AP,chi2)? False
      [-e] regular=False  chi=2  #3cyc=8  ==R_m(AP,chi2)? False

==============================================================================
READING
==============================================================================
  If chi(R_m)=2 for all m while Paley/QR has chi=3, the AP-rotational is the UNIQUE
  minimally-cyclic (chi=2) regular orbit, and Paley is a genuinely-more-cyclic (chi=3)
  one that VT and 3-cycle-count cannot distinguish but chi can. If every LRC tight config
  realizes the chi=2 (R_m) orbit (not chi=3 Paley), then the LRC extremal is precisely the
  MINIMALLY-cyclic regular tournament -- not the maximally-symmetric Paley -- and chi=2 is
  a clean characterization of the tight orbit. Any tight config landing on chi=3 would be a
  regular-but-not-AP tight config with a DIFFERENT chi (the user's target).
